WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.860
Welcome back to Models &amp; Agents, Episode 40 from May 5th, 2026.

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:12.120
Plenty of model releases and agent benchmarks moving today. OpenAI gives 8,000 developers

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:20.900
a month of 10 times Codex rate limits after the GPT 5.5 party sold out. OpenAI turned its

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:25.820
over-subscribed developer event into a broad giveaway that runs through June 5th.

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:34.020
The company emailed more than 8,000 developers who applied for the invite only GPT 5.5 gathering.

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:39.180
Everyone who signed up, whether accepted, waitlisted, or rejected,

00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:44.780
received an immediate 10 times increase in Codex rate limits on their personal accounts.

00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:52.500
Codex itself reportedly handled registration and even suggested the May 5th date and format for

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:54.640
the low-key San Francisco Meetup.

00:00:57.320 --> 00:01:03.100
The practical effect is a full month of high-volume agentic coding at no extra cost,

00:01:03.380 --> 00:01:09.120
timed against, andthropics competing code with clawed events. Developers are already reporting

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:14.800
the boost feels like a meaningful shift in daily workflow. Questions remain about whether

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:22.420
the new limits stack with the $200 pro tiers existing multiplier. Both OpenAI and Anthropic

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:28.380
now compete directly for developer mindshare through access and tooling rather than announcements alone.

00:01:28.820 --> 00:01:34.920
This rate limit window gives builders a concrete chance to stress-test agentic coding patterns

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:40.880
without watching token counters. If you have been rationing calls to stay under limits,

00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:49.280
this month removes that friction. DeepSeek version 4 pro just tied recent GPT 5.2 performance

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:56.300
on a 30-day persistent memory food truck benchmark. It achieved the result while running roughly

00:01:56.300 --> 00:02:04.900
17 times cheaper than the OpenAI model. The benchmark runs a simulated food truck through 34 tools

00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:11.600
with persistent memory and daily reflection. DeepSeek became the first Chinese model to reach

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:18.480
frontier tier placement on this evaluation. Consistency metrics also favorite with zero loans

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:27.100
lower food waste and tighter outcome variants across runs. Jiami's Mimo V2.5 pro joined the leaderboard

00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:36.240
shortly afterward at number six. Two Chinese models now sit inside the top six at under $3.50 per run.

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:42.820
Tuan released an official FP8 variant of its 27 billion parameter model.

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:52.160
The setup keeps a full 200,000 token BF-16 KV cache resident on a single 48 gigabyte card.

00:02:52.460 --> 00:02:59.560
It delivers 60 to 90 tokens per second with MTP equals two speculative decoding.

00:03:00.260 --> 00:03:07.960
Weight stay an FP8 while the KV cache remains in higher precision. Long agentic sessions therefore

00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:14.420
avoid the compounding errors that appear when both are quantized. The configuration delivers usable

00:03:14.420 --> 00:03:20.880
interactive coding performance without early context compaction. An anonymous model called

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:25.700
peanut debuted at number eight in the artificial analysis text to image arena.

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:32.900
Wates are expected soon positioning it as the strongest open weights image model ahead of flux

00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:40.260
twin knives and twin knives. Early indications suggest it suppresses current open checkpoints on

00:03:40.260 --> 00:03:46.680
prompted hearings and visual quality. It could become a near term high quality local alternative

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:56.340
for image generation workflows. A pure C++ GGML port of Microsoft's vibe voice now runs TTS with

00:03:56.340 --> 00:04:03.680
voice cloning and long form ASR with speaker diurization. The single binary supports CPU,

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:13.440
CUDA, metal, and Vulcan backends. The 0.5 billion real-time TTS model accepts 30 second reference clips

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:22.500
and outputs 24 kilohertz cloned speech. The 7 billion ASR model returns JSON segments with time stamps

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:30.280
and speaker labels up to 17 minutes of audio. Preconverted GGUF models are already available on

00:04:30.280 --> 00:04:37.620
hugging face. The project integrates as a local AI backend with no Python dependency at inference time.

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:45.820
A benchmark across 15 models and over 6100 test cases showed a simple defense reaching 100%

00:04:45.820 --> 00:04:53.480
on several models. Rapping untrusted content in a 128-bit random delimiter plus a short

00:04:53.480 --> 00:05:02.540
commanding instruction lifted defense rates dramatically. Gemma 4 moved from 21.6% to 100%.

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:11.520
GROC 3 mini fast and twin 2.5.7b reached the same perfect score. The strict prompt template

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:17.540
outperformed longer contextual reasoning prompts. The full data set and evaluation harness

00:05:17.540 --> 00:05:24.700
are public on hugging face and GitHub for immediate integration. The Fido Alliance has formed new working

00:05:24.700 --> 00:05:31.040
groups to define standards for payments initiated by autonomous AI agents. The effort targets

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:37.520
authentication and attestation flows that let agents complete transactions without exposing long lived

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:43.820
credentials. Early participation remains open to implementers building agentic payment flows.

00:05:44.680 --> 00:05:52.840
A developer is using GRPO on a 3 node MLX cluster to train 350-500 million parameter models.

00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:58.480
The models must output exactly 64 tokens for reddit post summarization.

00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:07.260
The setup runs synchronous parameters server training with VLLM-metal rollouts on worker nodes

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:15.600
and LLLM as a judge, pipeline scores faithfulness, coverage, conciseness, and clarity.

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:22.440
Length constrained training from an already fine-tuned checkpoint improves stability over training

00:06:22.440 --> 00:06:33.280
from scratch. Users report that twin 3.627bq8kxl begins repeating output once context exceeds roughly

00:06:33.280 --> 00:06:42.180
100,000 tokens. The issue appears even with 200,000 context enabled and Ngram speculative decoding

00:06:42.180 --> 00:06:50.880
active. Suggested mitigations include lowering context to 131,000 tokens and enabling checkpointing

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:58.460
every 8,000 tokens. A bill requiring age verification and disclosures for AI chatbots has been

00:06:58.460 --> 00:07:04.860
unanimously advanced to the Senate floor. The legislation frames the measures around child safety

00:07:04.860 --> 00:07:12.520
but introduces mandatory verification infrastructure. Local open-weight deployments remain unaffected.

00:07:13.180 --> 00:07:20.480
Okay, let's pop the hood on mixed precision KV caching for a moment. Everyone talks about quantizing

00:07:20.480 --> 00:07:26.640
the KV cache as if it were a simple toggle. In practice, the decision splits weight precision

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:33.440
from attention state precision. The KV cache grows linearly with context length while weights

00:07:33.440 --> 00:07:43.740
stay fixed. Keeping the cache in BF16 or FP16 while weights sit in FP8 or Q4KM prevents small

00:07:43.740 --> 00:07:50.760
per-step rounding errors. Those errors compound across agentic loops and multi-turn tool use.

00:07:51.720 --> 00:08:01.920
On a 40-hour gigabyte card, this split lets a 20,000-billion model hold 200,000 tokens of BF16 KV,

00:08:02.580 --> 00:08:11.440
plus the model itself. The cache occupies roughly 1.09 times the memory of an equivalent FP8 cache.

00:08:11.820 --> 00:08:18.800
It avoids the early context eviction that quantize KV forces. The latency cost is modest,

00:08:18.800 --> 00:08:26.120
typically 10-20% slower pre-fill than a fully quantized cache. The quality gain shows up as

00:08:26.120 --> 00:08:32.100
reduced repetition and more stable tool calling once context passes 80,000 tokens.

00:08:33.520 --> 00:08:39.960
The gacha that bites most teams is assuming the same quant recipe works for both short chats and long

00:08:39.960 --> 00:08:45.840
running agents. Persistent memory across dozens of tool calls needs the higher precision cache

00:08:45.840 --> 00:08:52.860
until the next generation of attention kernels arrives. If you have not tried the Vive Voice.cpp

00:08:52.860 --> 00:08:59.620
binary yet, grab a 30-second reference clip and test 0-shot voice cloning TTS this week.

00:09:00.200 --> 00:09:08.460
It runs on metal or kuda with no Python stack required. Add a 120-second 8-bit random

00:09:08.460 --> 00:09:14.980
delimiter plus the short-strict prompt template to any rag pipeline handling untrusted web content.

00:09:15.580 --> 00:09:22.640
The benchmark shows it lifts several 7-9 billion local models to 100% defense rate.

00:09:23.420 --> 00:09:35.660
Run 23.627 BFP8 through VLLM with BF16 KV cache on a 48 gigabyte card if you need 150,000

00:09:35.660 --> 00:09:44.320
plus token agent sessions, compared deep-seq V4 Pro against GPT 5.2 on your own multi-step agent

00:09:44.320 --> 00:09:51.460
tasks using the food truck bench harness. You can check whether the 17 times price difference holds

00:09:51.460 --> 00:10:01.380
for your workload. Experiment with GRPO length constrained fine-tuning on a 350-500 million model

00:10:01.380 --> 00:10:06.480
for summarization tasks where output token count must stay deterministic.

